  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThursday...\n\nA shortwave trough will pass through on Thursday, giving us plenty \nof forcing for more widespread showers and thunderstorms. Greatest \ncoverage will once again be over the higher terrain, where \nwidespread precipitation will cause flash flooding concerns across \nall of our burn scars and other vulnerable areas. Meanwhile, another \nincoming front will spread precip to the eastern plains later in the \nday and into the evening hours. This will cause a complex, hazardous \nscenario for the Aspen Acres fire, due to a combination of large \nscale monsoon moisture over the mountains, and post-frontal \nupsloping over the plains. Winds and the placement of heavy rainfall \nwill be complex over the fire and surrounding areas of the \nmountains/plains interface. As such, have issued a Flash Flood \nWatch. \n\nFriday...\n\nActivity lessens a bit on Friday, with the high pressure \nreorganizing over the Four Corners. Shower and thunderstorm activity \nwill remain plentiful over the mountains during the afternoon, \nmeaning another day of moderate to heavy rainfall, but overall the \neastern plains appear more stable, with only some isolated showers \nanticipated. Even so, burn scar flash flood concerns will continue. \nMeanwhile, temperatures will drop a few degrees, but calling the day \n'cooler' may be a bit of a stretch, since we'll still be seeing mid-\nhigh 90s over the eastern plains and high-80s in the valleys.\n\n\nThis Weekend Onwards...\n\nAs the upper high moves overhead, flow weakens aloft. Forcing for \nstorms will significantly weaken, though recycling monsoon moisture \nwill be plentiful enough to keep afternoon showers and storms going \nover the mountains. Meanwhile, the plains will dry out again, with \ntriple-digit temperatures returning. Current long-range guidance \nsuggests a wetter and potentially cooler (or at least less hot) \npattern as we move into next week.",
